Preserve task requests through compaction

Host-delivered assignments use standalone tool-output envelopes.
Compaction previously discarded them while retaining older user text,
so a completed or superseded request could remain the visible task.

Retain admitted deliveries and instructions from the receiving agent's
ancestors alongside ordinary user inputs in source order.
Preserve their protocol identities and admission metadata across
checkpoints and rollout replay, and exempt admitted input from generic
tool-output trimming before remote compaction.
Admission evidence identifies the input; it does not confer authority.

All inputs share the existing chronological retention budgets.
Newer user instructions take priority over older deliveries, boundary
text can be shortened, and opaque boundary content can be omitted.
